Z||000001||Cooter||05-17-2000||11:09 PM||||Find the DynoCams website and get one of their "cheater" cams. Follow their advice on the amount of ignition timing to use. Measure the timing with a degree wheel on the output shaft and where the flywheel magnet passes by the trailing edge of the magneto. Get a set of low tension rings. Throw away the second ring. The top ring is a "file to fit" so you can set the end gap. Make it into a "gapless" ring. This is done by filing the top of one end of the ring and the bottom of the other side so that they overlap when compressed into the bore. Be patient- if the ends butt it will ruin the block! Make sure the valve lash is what the cam calls for. You can loose a lot of lift with excessive clearance between the lifter and the valve. A stiffer intake valve spring will help. Find a 4 horsepower lawnmower head. They are the same size as the 5 HP, but the combustion chamber is a lot smaller so you get more compression. Bosche makes a very good non-resister race plug that is in a hot enough heat range to stay pretty clean. A thin head gasket will also help with compression- dont use a copper one though. If you do any port work, do not enlarge the size of the ports! Just remove the casting flash and smooth up the upper left corner of the intake port.<BR>That is all I can think of right now. Happy Mowing!||||reg||1
